---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import FurtherReading from '../components/FurtherReading.astro';
import SeaBreak from '../components/SeaBreak.astro';
import { celestial, scenes } from '../config/images';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../components/CTABand.astro';
const crumbs = [{ label: 'Prepare for Your Reading', href: '/prepare-for-your-reading/' }];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Prepare for Your Reading"
  description="How to prepare for an astrology reading with Mo Lumen: bring the birth information you actually have, the questions that matter, and a comfortable place to talk."
  path="/prepare-for-your-reading/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Before we meet</p>
        <h1>Prepare for your reading</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          You do not need to study astrology or arrive with a perfect list of questions. Three
          practical things are enough to give us more room to spend the session on what matters.
        </p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={scenes.prepareReading.src}
        srcset={scenes.prepareReading.srcset}
        sizes="100vw"
        alt={scenes.prepareReading.alt}
        width={scenes.prepareReading.width}
        height={scenes.prepareReading.height}
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">The short checklist</p>
      <h2>Bring these three things.</h2>
      <div class="grid grid--3" style="margin-top: var(--space-6);">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>The birth information you actually have</h3>
          <p>Exact and documented is useful when available. Approximate or unknown is also useful information when it is stated plainly.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>One or two real questions</h3>
          <p>You do not need a long list. A question that is genuinely on your mind gives us a much better place to begin.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>A comfortable place to talk</h3>
          <p>Reliable audio, some privacy, water, and enough room in your schedule that you do not have to rush out immediately afterward.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="callout" style="margin-top: var(--space-7);">
        <p style="margin: 0;"><strong>You do not need astrology vocabulary.</strong> Knowing the astrology is my responsibility. Your job is to bring the life you are actually living.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SeaBreak image={celestial.clearedTable} />

  <section class="section section--sea">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>If your birth time is uncertain</h2>
      <p>
        Tell me how you know the time rather than turning an estimate into a fact. Birth time affects
        the ascendant, angles, houses, and other time-sensitive techniques, but uncertainty does not
        erase the rest of the chart. We can work with what is solid and leave the uncertain layers
        open.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you want to investigate first, the <a href="/birth-time/">birth time guide</a> explains
        where to look and what different levels of confidence mean. The
        <a href="/birth-time-toolkit/#confidence-check">confidence check</a> takes about ninety
        seconds and does not ask you to enter your birth data.
      </p>

      <h2>Think about what is really on your mind</h2>
      <p>
        “Why does every job start well and then begin to feel wrong?” gives us more to work with than
        “Tell me about my career.” Specific questions help me understand where your attention already
        is; they do not lock the reading into a script.
      </p>

      <h2>During the session</h2>
      <p>
        You can ask questions, interrupt, disagree, add context, or simply listen while the picture
        develops. You will receive a recording, so you do not need to capture every sentence while
        we are talking.
      </p>

      <h2>Afterward</h2>
      <p>
        A second listen can be useful because you are no longer hearing everything for the first
        time. If you can, leave a little breathing room after the session rather than scheduling
        something demanding immediately afterward.
      </p>
      <FurtherReading />
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="That is enough preparation."
    body="Bring the birth information you actually have, what is on your mind, and yourself. I will take care of the astrology."
    buttonLabel="See How Readings Work"
    buttonHref="/how-readings-work/"
    secondaryLabel="Review the FAQs"
    secondaryHref="/frequently-asked-questions/"
  />
</BaseLayout>
